/bin/
# `go build ./tools/mutants` from here writes this, and once wrote it into a
# commit. The Makefile runs the tool with `go run`, so the binary is never
# wanted.
/mutants

# The other two tools are package main as well, and the Makefile runs them with
# `go run` too. `go build ./tools/bounds` left one of these in the tree while
# the tests for that tool were being written, which is how the gap was noticed:
# two of the three were covered and the reason applies to all of them.
/bounds
/deletions
/herdrcheck

# And where they land when the build is run from the tool's OWN directory,
# which is the ordinary way to build one: `cd tools/mutants && go build` names
# the binary after the directory and writes it there, not at the root. The
# entries above are root-anchored and cover none of these -- a 4 MB
# tools/mutants/mutants turned up untracked during the tool sweeps once, and
# reproducing it is one command.
/tools/mutants/mutants
/tools/bounds/bounds
/tools/deletions/deletions
/tools/herdrcheck/herdrcheck

# `go build .` from here writes this, and once got as far as a tagged release
# commit before anybody looked. The Makefile and build.sh both build to bin/,
# so a binary with this name is never wanted.
/herdr-remote-panes

# Working notes, kept locally rather than published.
/notes/

# The release notes, which docs/development.md has you write here and hand to
# `git tag -F` and `gh release create --notes-file`. Nothing in that procedure
# removes it, so it sits in the root untracked -- at the one moment this
# repository has already lost that argument twice, the entries above being what
# is left of it. `/notes/` does not cover it: these patterns are root-anchored
# and that one is a directory.
/notes.md
